# The answers:
#   0 + coordinates - it is there, and where it has to be
#   4 + coordinates - in the band, never clear of its edges while the list still
#                     moved; the position is live
#   5 + coordinates - it came into view and the swipes took it out again; the
#                     position is the LAST SIGHTING and is stale. Saying "not on
#                     screen" of an element that was on it is a false reason, and
#                     a false reason is worse than a vague one.
#   6               - the list ended in that direction and the element is not in it
#   7               - the swipes ran out and the list was still moving
#   8               - the swipes ran out on a screen whose dump shows no list, so
#                     where the list ends could not be seen
#   9 + coordinates - it is on the screen, the list ended, and it is still not
#                     clear of the edge; the position is live
#
# The end of a list is read from the list, not from the screen. Two reads of the
# whole screen that come out the same do not say the list ended: a swipe that
# starts on a carousel, a slider or a map is taken by that element, the list
# stays where it was, and the screen looks exactly like the end. And two reads
# that differ do not say it moved: on Android the dump carries the status bar,
# and its clock ticks. So each read picks the list the search runs along - the
# element that says it scrolls on that axis (list_probe) - and remembers the
# first and the last row of it that are on the screen, by label and position.
# Nothing outside that list is compared, the clock included.
#
# One swipe that moved nothing is not yet the end: it may have landed on
# something inside the list that took the gesture. The swipes start away from
# every element inside the list that scrolls on its own, and when one still moves
# nothing, a second, shorter and slower one goes out from a different point. Only
# when that one moves nothing either is it the end. A false end then needs two
# misses in a row, from two points, both inside the list and both clear of what
# scrolls inside it.
#
# A list cannot be counted, which is why the rows at its ends are compared
# instead: a long list is virtualised, and the dump holds only the rows on the
# screen. The last row on the screen and where it stands are always there.
#
# A screen whose dump shows no list gets the old search: down the middle, eight
# swipes (SCROLL_TRIES_BLIND). It says so once, and its miss says the swipes ran
# out on a screen with no list - not that the list ended.
scroll_to(){ local lbl="$1" dir="${2:-down}" clear="${3:-}" verb="${4:-scrollToText}" i=0 xy c lo hi blo bhi m w h seen="" nudge st probe prev="" ctl=0 ended="" flat="" kb=""
